# How to Use This Document

## What this topic covers

Topic 1 is the foundation of the whole subject. It joins two syllabus heads. The examiner treats them as one family.

- **Head I — Introduction to Gender Studies:** what Gender Studies is; how it differs from Women's Studies; why it uses many disciplines; the autonomy versus integration debate; and the state of Gender Studies in Pakistan.
- **Head II — Social Construction of Gender:** the history of the idea that gender is "made"; queer theory and the question "is sex made by society too?"; masculinities and femininities; and the nature versus culture debate.

The topic also teaches three things the examiner has asked that the syllabus does not name: **gendered language** (2020), the **many meanings of the word "gender"** (2020), and the **transgender law** of 2018 and its court case (2026).

This is the second-heaviest topic in the record: ==16.5 visits== in eleven papers. Four families of questions — Women's vs Gender Studies; the many disciplines; autonomy vs integration; the field in Pakistan — have been asked fourteen times between them. If you master this topic, you will find at least one strong question in almost every paper.

## How this document is built

1. **Part Zero** gives you the words of the topic and a one-page picture of it.
2. **Part One** has eleven chapters. Chapters 1–5 teach the discipline (Head I). Chapters 6–11 teach the social construction of gender (Head II).
3. **Part Two** sets out today's debates on the topic.
4. **Part Three** lists every past question, what the record shows, and five likely questions.
5. **Part Four** is a bank of 120 numbered one-liners, written in the style of the past MCQs.
6. **Part Five** is the revision sheet — the whole topic in a few pages, with every word explained, for the night before the exam.

Evidence always sits inside the point it proves. A **definition** card is teal, a **thinker** card plum, a **data** card blue, a **law** card navy and a **case** card coral. Each card ends with an arrow (➜) that says what the evidence proves.

::: simple
Read Chapters 1–5 as one story: how a new subject was born and how it lives in Pakistan. Read Chapters 6–11 as a second story: how one big idea — that gender is made, not born — was found, argued over and applied.
:::

# Part Zero — The ABC of Topic 1

## 0.1  The words of this topic

::: table | The key terms of Topic 1 (★ = you must be able to define it in one sentence).
@widths 3.3,10.7
Term | Simple meaning, with an example
Sex ★ | The **body** differences between females and males: chromosomes, hormones and organs. *Only women can give birth.*
Gender ★ | The **roles, behaviour and power** that society gives to being female or male. *The belief that only mothers should raise babies.*
Gender Studies ★ | The study of **how society makes gender**, how gender shares out power, and how it mixes with class, religion and other identities.
Women's Studies ★ | The field, started around 1969–70, that puts **women** at the centre of study.
Men's / masculinity studies | The study of men as gendered people — how "manhood" is made and what it costs.
Discipline | A recognised branch of knowledge with its own subject, methods, departments and journals.
Multidisciplinary ★ | Using **several disciplines side by side** to study one subject.
Interdisciplinary | **Mixing** the methods of several disciplines into one new approach.
Autonomy ★ | Keeping Gender Studies as a **separate department** with its own staff and degrees.
Integration ★ | **Adding gender to every subject** — law, medicine, economics and so on. Also called mainstreaming.
Androcentrism ★ | Treating **men as the normal human** and women as the exception.
Patriarchy ★ | A system in which **men hold most power** and this is treated as natural.
Essentialism ★ | The view that women and men have a **fixed, natural nature**.
Social constructionism ★ | The view that things which look natural are really **made by society**.
Socialisation ★ | **Learning** society's rules, including gender rules, from family, school, media and religion.
Gender role ★ | The **behaviour society expects** of each gender. *Men as breadwinners.*
Gender identity ★ | A person's **inner sense** of being a woman, a man, both or neither.
Gender stereotype | A fixed, over-simple belief about a gender. *"Women are emotional."*
Femininity / masculinity ★ | The **qualities society expects** of women / of men.
Hegemonic masculinity ★ | Connell's term for the **most honoured way of being a man**, which supports men's power over women and over other men.
Emphasised femininity | Connell's term for the kind of femininity built around **pleasing and serving men**.
Androgyny | Having **both** "masculine" and "feminine" traits (Sandra Bem, 1974).
Queer theory ★ | A theory (from about 1990) that **questions fixed boxes** of sex, gender and sexuality.
Heteronormativity | The assumption that there are **only two opposite genders** and that only heterosexuality is normal.
Performativity ★ | Judith Butler's idea that **gender is created by repeated acts**, not by an inner core.
Intersex | Born with a body that **does not fit the usual male or female pattern**. Islamic law calls such a person *khuntha*.
Transgender / khawaja sira ★ | A person whose **gender identity differs from the sex recorded at birth**. *Khawaja sira* is the traditional South Asian term.
Nature versus culture ★ | The debate over whether gender differences come from **biology** or from **upbringing and society**.
Biological determinism | The belief that **biology alone decides** behaviour and roles.
Deconstruction | **Taking an idea apart** to show its hidden assumptions and rankings (Jacques Derrida).
Gendered language ★ | Language that **carries gender bias** in its words, grammar or use. *"Chairman", "mankind".*
:::

## 0.2  The one distinction that starts everything

::: fig compare | Sex and gender: the distinction on which the whole subject rests.
@vs vs
## Sex — the body
- Chromosomes, hormones, organs
- Given at birth; mostly the same everywhere
- Changes very little over history
- Studied by biology and medicine
## Gender — society
- Roles, rules, identities, power
- Learnt; different in different places
- Changes from one generation to the next
- Studied by the social sciences
:::

For fifty years this table has been the first thing every student of the subject learns. Topic 1 teaches you to use it. Then, in Chapter 8, it teaches you to question it, because some thinkers argue that even the left-hand column is partly shaped by society.

## 0.3  Topic 1 on one page

::: fig grid | Topic 1 on one page: eleven chapters, two stories.
@cols 3
Ch 1 · What Gender Studies is: meaning, scope, why it was needed
Ch 2 · Women's vs Gender Studies: history; eight differences; how they relate
Ch 3 · Many disciplines: what each one adds
Ch 4 · Autonomy vs integration: separate department or part of every subject?
Ch 5 · Gender Studies in Pakistan: 1989 centres; gains; problems
Ch 6 · Constructionism: history of the idea; seven theories
Ch 7 · Meanings of "gender": eight meanings; gendered language
Ch 8 · Queer theory: is sex made by society too?
Ch 9 · Masculinities and femininities: Connell; where roles come from
Ch 10 · Nature versus culture: biology, society, both
Ch 11 · The transgender debate: 2009, 2018, 2023
Parts 2–5 · Debates, record, one-liners, revision sheet
:::

::: eye | How the examiner uses this topic
The examiner asks the discipline half (Chapters 1–5) as "define and differentiate" and "write a note / give your views" questions. He or she often joins two families in one question — for example, the field in Pakistan *plus* autonomy versus integration. The construction half (Chapters 6–11) is asked as theory: "what are the theories…", "deconstruct…", "natural or social?" and, most recently, theory applied to Pakistani law. Learn each chapter so that it can stand alone *and* be joined to another.
:::
